{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CartesianProductView","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Vertices"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are the pairs "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pair(g, h)"},{"type":"text","text":". Vertex (g, h) has index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(g)·|V(H)| + index(h)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row-major, the numbering of NamedGraphs’ grids, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertex(atIndex:)"},{"type":"text","text":" costs one division and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" one lookup in each factor."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Rows"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" list the G-moves first, ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g′, h)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each entry g′ of G’s row of g, then the H-moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". They are computed from the factors’ index rows and never hash. A factor’s self-loop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gives a product self-loop, listed twice in an undirected row as the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" law asks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Edges"}]},{"text":" are named by the factor edge and the factor vertex that generate them:","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first(edge: e, vertexIndex: h)"},{"text":" is G’s edge ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"text":" at H’s vertex index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h"},{"text":", and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: e)"},{"text":" is H’s edge ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"text":" at G’s vertex index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"text":" lists every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first"},{"text":" edge, G-edge outer, then every ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second"},{"text":" edge, G-vertex outer. When both factors have","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge indices, so does the product: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·|V(H)| + h"},{"text":", then ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"m_G·|V(H)| + g·m_H + index(e)"},{"text":".","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Products of multigraphs and pseudographs are defined edge by edge, so ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount"},{"text":", the rows and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the degrees always agree.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"To materialize it, pass it to a representation’s initializer. Products are ","type":"text"},{"code":"Sendable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when both","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"factors are.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"title":"CartesianProductView","symbolKind":"struct","externalID":"s:13GraphProducts20CartesianProductViewV","roleHeading":"Structure","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"CartesianProductView"